Offer a picture on the Android clipboard to X clients.

Upstream's clipboard bridge is text only: the server takes the CLIPBOARD selection when the host
announces a clip, lists STRING, TEXT and UTF8_STRING in TARGETS, and answers a request with the
text the host sends. A screenshot copied on the phone could not be pasted into an X program. The
Java side cannot do this alone: the selection owner is the server itself (lorie's
`lorieOwnSelection`), and only the owner can name targets and fill the requestor's property.
Running `xclip` inside the session as a second owner was the alternative, and would hold the
picture in a client for as long as it owned the selection.

So the owner learns picture targets. `lorie.h` gains three events sharing one payload struct:

- `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_ANNOUNCE_TARGETS` (host to server): whether the clip has text, then the image
  targets one per line (the clip's own type and `image/png`, chosen by `LorieView`). The server
  interns them (at most `LORIE_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_TARGETS_MAX`) and takes the selection as the plain
  announce does. TARGETS lists the text targets only when the clip has text, then the image
  targets; a text request on a clip with no text is refused. Upstream's `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_ANNOUNCE`
  is unchanged and still means "text, no picture", so text paste is exactly as before. Every
  announce, either kind, moves the clipboard serial on.
- `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_REQUEST` (server to host): an X client asked for an image target; the
  frame carries the current clipboard serial and the target. The request is queued, and asked of
  the host once per target however many clients wait on it. `activity.cpp` calls
  `LorieView.requestClipboardImage(String, int)`, resolved in `nativeInit` with `FindMethodOrDie`.
- `EVENT_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_CHUNK` (host to server): one piece of the answer. Each frame carries the
  serial the request brought, a last-chunk flag, the target name and at most
  `LORIE_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_CHUNK` (64 KiB) of picture bytes. A last chunk with no bytes is a refusal.
  The input thread reads each chunk whole and hands it to the X server thread with
  `QueueWorkProc`, as every other clipboard event is handed over; the X server thread alone
  assembles them, so no assembly state crosses threads. Chunks whose serial is not the current one
  answer a clip that has since been replaced and are dropped. At most one picture is assembled at
  a time; it is freed, and the requesters waiting on it refused, on the next announce, on a chunk
  for another target, and when the host disconnects. `LORIE_CLIPBOARD_IMAGE_MAX` (32 MiB) caps the
  running total: past it the rest is drained and dropped and the requesters are refused. On the
  last chunk every request waiting on that target is answered — the property typed as the target,
  format 8 — and the bytes are freed at once: a picture is never cached, unlike upstream's text.

Chunks, not one frame, because the host writes on its main thread like every other event (the
socket is one stream; a second writer would interleave) and one 32 MiB write held that thread for
as long as the server took to read it. `LorieView` posts one chunk per main-thread message, so
other events go between chunks in order, and sends one picture's chunks before reading the next
picture. `sendClipboardImageChunk` and `sendClipboardAnnounceTargets` write their whole frame or
drop the connection through the hangup path `xcallback` already has (a frame cut short would
leave the server reading the next event from its middle); the chunk call returns false then and
`LorieView` sends nothing more. Both are plain natives, not `@FastNative`, since they can wait.

No INCR: INCR exists for an owner whose ChangeProperty would pass the maximum request size, and
this owner is the server, which writes the property directly; the requestor reads it with one
GetProperty, whose reply length has no such limit.

A requester must never be left waiting. Waiting requests are refused when the next announce
arrives, when the host disconnects, when there is no host to ask, and when the answer cannot be
written. A request holds its ClientPtr until it is answered, so a `ClientStateCallback`
registered beside upstream's `SelectionCallback` drops a client's waiting requests when it goes
(ClientStateGone) or is retained (ClientStateRetained): the dispatcher frees the client right
after. Upstream's text requests (`lorieDataTargetHead`) had the same dangling pointer, and the
same callback forgets them too.

The picture's frames are larger than one read, so the server reads them with a read loop
(`lorieReadFully`) and the host writes them with a write loop that waits out a full buffer
(`lorieWriteFully`, at most a second per wait).

Applies after 0001-0003; it extends the event enum, the `nativeInit` block and the event loops
those patches touch. `LorieView` must carry `@Keep requestClipboardImage(String, int)`,
`sendClipboardAnnounceTargets` and `sendClipboardImageChunk`, or the process dies on the first
view, so the Java half ships in the same commit as this patch and the prebuilts built from it.

See x11-server/UPSTREAM.md.

